# Findings — SYN Flood DoS Attack

## Attack Identification

- IP 203.0.113.0 appears in hundreds of consecutive log entries — far beyond normal traffic volume
- Every packet from this IP is a **SYN packet only** — the defining fingerprint of a SYN Flood
- No SYN-ACK responses or final ACKs were ever sent by the attacker
- Attack begins at log entry #52 and continues relentlessly

## Impact on the Server

- Server dutifully responds to each SYN with a SYN-ACK and reserves a backlog slot
- Half-open connections accumulate as the attacker never completes handshakes
- TCP backlog table saturates — no capacity for new legitimate connections
- Server begins sending RST, ACK to legitimate clients (entries #73, #77, #80, #85, #92, #121)
- 504 Gateway Timeout error first appears at entry **#77**

## Impact on Legitimate Users

- Early log entries (#47–#51) show legitimate users (`198.51.100.23`, `198.51.100.14`) successfully completing handshakes
- Once the backlog fills, real users are either ignored or forcibly disconnected
- Web service becomes completely unavailable — full denial of service

## Protocol Details

- **Attack target:** Port 443 (HTTPS) on server 192.0.2.1
- **Protocol exploited:** TCP — specifically the connection establishment (SYN) phase
- **Root vulnerability:** TCP backlog table has finite size; server must reserve resources before handshake completion
- **Key flaw exploited:** Normal TCP behaviour — the server is doing exactly what it's designed to do
